.TH n3n 8  "1 Oct 2026" "version 3" "SUPERUSER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
n3n \- the n3n peer-to-peer VPN, edge and supernode in one program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B n3n edge
[\fIoptions..\fR] [command] [\fIcommand args\fR]
.br
.B n3n supernode
[\fIoptions..\fR] [command] [\fIcommand args\fR]
.br
.B n3n-edge
[\fIoptions..\fR] [command] [\fIcommand args\fR]
.br
.B n3n-supernode
[\fIoptions..\fR] [command] [\fIcommand args\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
n3n is a peer-to-peer VPN system - it is the LAN you have when you have noLAN.

Both daemons of n3n are one program.  It takes the role of the name it is
started as: installed as
.B n3n-edge
and
.BR n3n-supernode ,
links to
.B n3n
(copies on Windows), it is that daemon, with the same options, commands and
configuration.  Started as
.BR n3n ,
the first argument names the role:
.B n3n edge start
is the same as
.BR "n3n-edge start" .
Without a role, it lists the roles it has.
.TP
.B edge
A node of the virtual network, with a TAP device: see
.BR n3n-edge (8).
.TP
.B supernode
The introduction registry and relay of the edges: see
.BR n3n-supernode (8).
A supernode can be an edge of one community too, with
\fBsupernode.tap\fR=true.
.PP
A build with
.B ./configure --disable-relay
has the edge only: no
.B n3n-supernode
then, and
.B n3n
knows only the role
.BR edge .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B n3n edge start mynetwork
the same as \fBn3n-edge start mynetwork\fR
.TP
.B n3n supernode -h
the help of the supernode
.SH EXIT STATUS
2 if no role was given or found, otherwise that of the role.
.SH SEE ALSO
n3n-edge(8) n3n-supernode(8) n3n(7)
.br
the documentation found in n3n's \fBdocs/\fR folder, also at
https://github.com/n42n/n3n/tree/main/docs
